WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.410
This is a Wendover Productions video in collaboration
with Real Life Lore and made possible by Lootcrate.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:07.960
Is the European Union a country?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:12.550
It’s a bit of strange question, with an
easy answer but a difficult explanation.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:13.670
The answer is no.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:18.080
The European Union is not a country, but…
you can move between countries without passing

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.480
through border control, work between countries
without a Visa, and they use the same currency,

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.980
and there are elections to a single parliament,
and there’s a single government, and there

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.850
are official languages, and a single economic
market, a single aviation market, and… this

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:32.880
is beginning to sound awfully like… a country.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.309
Let’s do a 90 second recap of how the EU
works.

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:38.960
This is the European Union.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.260
There are 28 member states including the UK
which has voted to leave the EU, but just

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.590
hasn’t yet gone through the process to leave.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.790
Of those, these are in the Schengen Zone meaning
that there are no border controls between

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:50.800
them.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.960
That means that a typical border crossing
in the EU looks like this.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.740
These four are legally obliged to be in the
Schengen Zone but just aren’t, and these

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.660
two have opt-outs in their treaties that exempt
them from being in the Schengen Zone.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.390
These countries are part of the Eurozone meaning
the euro is their sole legal currency.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.830
These guys are obliged to join the Eurozone
once they reach a certain economic target,

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.850
which they haven’t, and these two have opt-outs
exempting them from the Eurozone.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.960
Each member country of the European Union
elects its own Members of the European Parliament,

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.729
known as MEPs, but the Parliament can’t
make laws by itself.

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:27.830
Laws are proposed by the European Commission,
who kinda work like an executive branch.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:31.979
They then go to the Parliament who, if they
approve it, send it to the council of the

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:33.110
European Union.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:37.080
While the Parliament represents the people
of the European Union since the MEPs are elected

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.820
by direct election, the Council represents
the Governments since its made up of a rotating

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.470
roster of national ministers.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:48.270
If a proposed piece of legislation makes it
through both the Parliament and Council of

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.409
Europe, it becomes law.

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:55.181
So that’s how the European Union works,
at least a massively simplified version, but

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:59.770
how do countries work… or rather, what makes
a country a country.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.630
Well calling a country a country is a bit
misleading because the word “country”

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.630
can mean a lot of things.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.539
What you’re probably thinking of when I
say country is sovereign states—France,

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:13.000
Japan, the US, etc—but there are non-sovereign
states that are countries.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.260
Scotland is a country, indisputably, but it’s
not a sovereign state.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.060
It’s a part of the Sovereign State of the
United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.060
Ireland.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.930
So, once again, what makes a country a country?

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:30.000
Well, there’s something called the Declaratory
Theory of Statehood that sets out four criteria

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.290
for statehood.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.330
The first is “a permanent population”—a
country is not a country without people so

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.830
it needs people in its territory permanently.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.000
The EU easily has this.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.940
More than half a billion people live within
its borders.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.830
If it were a country, it would be the third
most populous on earth and have one of the

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.740
second highest gdps in the world.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.550
The second requirement is a defined territory.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:57.099
It’s a common misconception that a new country
can only form on unclaimed territory—according

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:02.160
to the declaratory theory a sovereign state
can be created in an area where another sovereign

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.640
state already exists.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.390
Just look at North and South Korea—both
claim the territory of each other and yet

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.990
they’re both sovereign states.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:12.830
The European Union has a territory, but its
a bit fuzzy.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:18.030
Any territory that you can call EU territory
is also territory of other entities, the countries

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.360
that make up the EU.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.569
But that doesn’t necessarily stop the EU
from having a territory.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:27.360
Going back to the example of the UK, the official
sovereign state—the United Kingdom of Great

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.239
Britain and Northern Ireland—really doesn’t
have any of its own territory.

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:36.070
Any territory of the UK is part of England,
Wales, Scotland, or Northern Ireland—each

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.069
countries by themselves.

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:40.379
The US is in a similar situation.

00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:44.260
States act kinda-like mini-countries and there
really is no federal territory that is not

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.000
part of a state’s territory.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.260
Especially given its open border policy, the
EU’s territory is in function indistinguishable

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.610
from that of any sovereign state.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:57.590
The third requirement is a government and
the government can’t just be a puppet of

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.310
another sovereign state.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.400
The government needs to have whats called
“an essential core of independence.”

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.390
As I’ve mentioned, the EU has a government
but for a few reasons the EU government is

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.560
different from that a sovereign state.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.200
Here’s the problem, the EU’s government
is not independent.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.079
The power comes from below, as in the power
comes from the member states.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:23.840
The power of most sovereign states also comes
from below, but in that case its the people

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.280
that give a state power.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.540
In the case of the EU, since power is granted
by sovereign states, those states are really

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.390
above the EU in power and therefore the EU
government is a subservient government.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.870
That violates the criteria for a truly independent
government.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:44.301
Although, what’s the difference between
this and UK government then—the country

00:04:44.301 --> 00:04:45.301
of countries?

00:04:45.301 --> 00:04:50.210
The United Kingdom is also made up of countries
so isn’t their government subservient?

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:55.470
Well, in the EU, there is a system and structure
to leaving, whereas in the United Kingdom

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.060
or really any other country, the entities
within the sovereign states cannot leave without

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.610
a change in government structure and procedure.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:08.199
When parts of sovereign states leave sovereign
states, they do so typically without a legal

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:10.650
right but rather a moral right.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:15.030
It’s called “the Right to Revolution”
according to the philosopher John Locke.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:19.600
When a government no longer serves the people,
as in it fails to protect the rights of the

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.540
people or becomes the entity that people need
protection from, there is a near universal

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.900
moral understanding that the people can either
overthrow or leave that government.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:34.700
Members of the EU can choose whether or not
to continue membership on a legal basis rather

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:35.970
than a moral one.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:40.240
The whole structure and system was set up
by the member states, so even though it is

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.639
overseeing the states, the power originates
from the states it oversees.

00:05:44.639 --> 00:05:50.620
The continued existence of the EU relies on
the will of its members to continue the system.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.610
That is never the case with an independent
government.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:57.780
That being said, while the origin of power
may be different, the EU government functions

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.360
in most ways like any other government.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.449
It has different branches, agencies, economic
systems, leaders, and more, so while its different,

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:10.639
the EU does partially fulfill the government
requirement for statehood.

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:14.590
The last criteria for statehood outlined in
the Declaratory Theory is “the capacity

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:19.289
to enter into relations with other states”
and the EU absolutely has this.

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:24.250
There are ambassadors to the EU, ambassadors
of the EU, embassies of the EU, embassies

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:29.610
to the EU, intergovernmental organizations
between the EU and non-EU countries, treaties

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.810
between the EU and non-EU countries, and more.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:37.590
While most foreign relations are handled by
individual member countries, there are absolutely

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.170
foreign relations of the EU as a whole.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:45.160
So, the European Union has fulfilled each
criteria for sovereign statehood on out list,

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.870
but it still isn’t a state.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:53.450
Here’s the problem: statehood, as in being
a sovereign country, is not a natural phenomenon.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.290
No part of nature creates countries.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:59.720
You can call salt salt if it’s made of Sodium
Chloride.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.020
That’s the requirement for salt being salt
and we can’t change that.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.130
That how nature makes salt.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.000
We created the idea of countries.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.700
They’re a social construct, so society decides
what is a sovereign country and what is not.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:17.770
While we can lay down a number of requisites
for statehood, they are just guidelines to

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:21.919
achieve the final goal—society’s acceptance
of a country.

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:26.550
We can’t just say these criteria make a
country a country unless individuals believe

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:31.311
in those criteria since countries, like all
social constructs, only work if there’s

00:07:31.311 --> 00:07:34.180
a collective belief and following of that
system.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.970
It’s similar to money.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:40.319
Money only works if everyone believes that
pieces of paper equal value.

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:45.300
Countries only work if everyone believes that
certain imaginary lines separate who and what

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.300
leads people.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.750
In the case of statehood, you can’t just
ask every person in society whether or not

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.199
they think a country is a sovereign country.

00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:58.389
There are socially accepted countries already
and therefore those act as a proxy for society

00:07:58.389 --> 00:08:01.319
to decide whether a country is sovereign or
not.

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:06.410
A countries sovereignty is judged off of how
many other sovereign countries recognize its

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:07.410
sovereignty.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:12.000
The European Union is not a sovereign state
because nobody accepts it as one.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.389
While it may function in many ways like a
sovereign country, it is not one because sovereignty

00:08:17.389 --> 00:08:19.930
is neither its goal or desire.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.330
This shows you just how difficult it is to
define what a country is.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:28.220
Supranational organizations like the EU act
like countries, but at the same time… so

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:32.310
do some subnational entities—as in parts
of countries.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:33.909
Most specifically in the US.

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:38.060
(Joseph’s part) States have a level of sovereignty
that blur the line between what is part of

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.860
a sovereign country and a county itself.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:44.010
What is the difference between a state in
the United States and a country.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.930
They fulfill almost all the criteria that
you just heard about so that’s why I asked

